{"step_index":96,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:57:34Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:57:34+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:57:34+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/lean_disproof.py`\nTotal Lines: 291\nTotal Bytes: 13556\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: \"\"\"\n2: pipeline/lean_disproof.py — formal Lean 4 *disproofs* for refuted conjectures.\n3: \n4: The refutation engine already produces the hard part of a disproof: a conjecture\n5: together with a graph on which it fails. Turning that pair into a machine-checked\n6: theorem costs nothing extra — the proof is \"evaluate both sides on this graph\" —\n7: provided the invariants can actually be evaluated by the kernel. They can, via\n8: the computable mirror in ``LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable`` (the\n9: specification-style definitions in ``GraphInvariants.lean`` use ``sInf`` over a\n10: ``Set`` and are noncomputable, so ``decide`` stalls on them).\n11: \n12: The emitted shape is a negated universal discharged at the witness::\n13: \n14:     theorem CEGIS_disproof_ab12 :\n15:         ¬ (∀ {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n16:              (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj],\n17:              GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G →\n18:              GraphCalc.totalZeroForcingNumber G ≤ GraphCalc.zeroForcingNumber G) := by\n19:       intro h\n20:       have := @h (Fin 5) _ _ Gcex _ (by decide)\n21:       revert this\n22:       decide\n23: \n24: Scope mirrors ``lean_export``: a conjecture whose invariants or classes are not\n25: in the computable layer is skipped rather than mis-formalized, and so is a\n26: witness too large to evaluate (the subset enumerations are exponential in the\n27: vertex count).\n28: \"\"\"\n29: from __future__ import annotations\n30: \n31: import hashlib\n32: import re\n33: from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n34: \n35: from pipeline import linear_form\n36: \n37: # invariant column → expression over `(G : SimpleGraph V)` in the computable layer.\n38: SUPPORTED: Dict[str, str] = {\n39:     \"order\":                         \"GraphCalc.order G\",\n40:     \"size\":                          \"GraphCalc.size G\",\n41:     \"minimum_degree\":                \"G.minDegree\",\n42:     \"maximum_degree\":                \"G.maxDegree\",\n43:     \"clique_number\":                 \"GraphCalc.cliqueNum G\",\n44:     \"independence_number\":           \"GraphCalc.indepNum G\",\n45:     \"domination_number\":             \"GraphCalc.dominationNumber G\",\n46:     \"independent_domination_number\": \"GraphCalc.independentDominationNumber G\",\n47:     \"slater\":                        \"GraphCalc.slaterNumber G\",\n48:     \"annihilation_number\":           \"GraphCalc.annihilationNumber G\",\n49:     \"zero_forcing_number\":           \"GraphCalc.zeroForcingNumber G\",\n50:     \"total_zero_forcing_number\":     \"GraphCalc.totalZeroForcingNumber G\",\n51:     # Computable mirror added alongside the spec-layer `sInf` version, which is\n52:     # noncomputable and so was unusable here; checked against graphcalc by\n53:     # tools/lean_differential.py before being listed.\n54:     \"connected_zero_forcing_number\": \"GraphCalc.connectedZeroForcingNumber G\",\n55:     \"vertex_cover_number\":            \"GraphCalc.vertexCoverNumber G\",\n56:     \"total_domination_number\":       \"GraphCalc.totalDominationNumber G\",\n57:     \"edge_cover_number\":             \"GraphCalc.edgeCoverNumber G\",\n58:     \"sub_total_domination_number\":   \"GraphCalc.subTotalDominationNumber G\",\n59:     \"roman_domination_number\":       \"GraphCalc.romanDominationNumber G\",\n60:     \"double_roman_domination_number\": \"GraphCalc.doubleRomanDominationNumber G\",\n61:     \"radius\":                        \"GraphCalc.radius G\",\n62: }\n63: \n64: # class column → decidable predicate in the computable layer.\n65: CLASS_PREDICATES: Dict[str, str] = {\n66:     \"nontrivial\":     \"GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass\",\n67:     \"regular\":        \"GraphCalc.IsRegularClass\",\n68:     \"cubic\":          \"GraphCalc.IsCubicClass\",\n69:     \"subcubic\":       \"GraphCalc.IsSubcubicClass\",\n70:     \"eulerian\":       \"GraphCalc.IsEulerianClass\",\n71:     \"triangle_free\":  \"GraphCalc.IsTriangleFreeClass\",\n72:     \"K_4_free\":       \"GraphCalc.IsK4FreeClass\",\n73:     \"connected\":      \"GraphCalc.IsConnectedClass\",\n74:     \"tree\":           \"GraphCalc.IsTreeClass\",\n75:     \"claw_free\":      \"GraphCalc.IsClawFreeClass\",\n76:     \"cograph\":        \"GraphCalc.IsCographClass\",\n77:     # Computable mirror of the spec-layer `G.Colorable 2`, which has no\n78:     # Decidable instance; validated against graphcalc before being listed.\n79:     \"bipartite\":      \"GraphCalc.IsBipartiteClass\",\n80: }\n81: \n82: # Every invariant is a minimum/maximum over subsets of V, so evaluation is\n83: # exponential in the vertex count, and past some size the kernel check stops\n84: # being worth attempting. The refutation itself is still recorded either way.\n85: #\n86: # The previous value of 8 turned out to measure the *heartbeat limit* rather\n87: # than feasibility: order-9 witnesses all failed at ~26 s with `maxHeartbeats`\n88: # exceeded, but one verified in 387 s once heartbeats were unbounded (see the\n89: # preamble). 10 is deliberately conservative — beyond it the cost has not been\n90: # measured, and an unbounded heartbeat count means a bad case hangs until the\n91: # subprocess timeout rather than failing fast.\n92: MAX_WITNESS_ORDER = 10\n93: \n94: _REL_LEAN = {\"≤\": \"≤\", \"≥\": \"≥\", \"<=\": \"≤\", \">=\": \"≥\", \"=\": \"=\"}\n95: \n96: # `maxRecDepth` raises the *elaborator's* recursion limit only; it does not\n97: # weaken the kernel check that follows. The whole proof is evaluation, and the\n98: # class tests enumerate vertex tuples: the cograph test is four nested\n99: # quantifiers, so an 8-vertex witness needs 8⁴ steps. Measured at n = 8 — the\n100: # default depth fails on cograph (connected/tree/claw-free still pass), this\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
