{"step_index":121,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:12Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:58:12+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:16+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Roman domination number\" \"diameter\" \"radius\" \"girth\"\" returned the following summary:\nIn graph theory, the **Roman domination number** ($\\gamma_R(G)$) is a variation of the classical domination number where vertices are labeled with 0, 1, or 2 such that every vertex labeled 0 is adjacent to at least one vertex labeled 2, and the total weight (sum of labels) is minimized[1][2].\n\nResearchers frequently study the relationships between the Roman domination number and other fundamental graph invariants such as **diameter**, **radius**, and **girth** to establish bounds and characterize specific graph classes[3][4][5].\n\n### Key Relationships and Bounds\n\n*   **Diameter ($\\text{diam}(G)$):** The diameter—the maximum distance between any two vertices—is often used to bound the Roman domination number[6][7]. For instance, research has shown that for connected graphs with a sufficiently large Roman domination number (e.g., $\\gamma_R(G) > 3$), there are upper bounds on the diameter[8]. Conversely, studies explore how the diameter and other parameters constrain the minimum weight of a Roman dominating function[3][4].\n*   **Girth ($\\text{gir}(G)$):** The girth—the length of the shortest cycle in a graph—is another parameter used to refine bounds on $\\gamma_R(G)$[9][7]. Specific results exist for graphs with restricted girth, such as triangle-free graphs (girth $\\geq 4$) or graphs with higher girth, where the Roman domination number is analyzed in relation to the graph's structure[10].\n*   **Radius ($\\text{rad}(G)$):** While less commonly paired directly with Roman domination than diameter, the radius (the minimum eccentricity of any vertex) is often investigated alongside diameter in extremal graph theory problems[7][11]. Bounds for domination-related parameters frequently incorporate radius to describe the \"spread\" of the graph[12][11].\n\n### Research Context\n*   **Extremal Problems:** Much of the literature focuses on finding graphs that satisfy equality in certain bounds (e.g., $\\gamma_R(G) = n - \\Delta + 1$) or characterizing graphs where the Roman domination number behaves in a specific way relative to their diameter and girth[1][10][8].\n*   **General Inequalities:** For any connected graph, the Roman domination number is bounded by the order of the graph ($n$), specifically $2 \\leq \\gamma_R(G) \\leq n$ (for $n \\geq 3$)[1][13]. More precise bounds often involve the maximum degree ($\\Delta$), minimum degree ($\\delta$), and the aforementioned distance-based parameters[8][14].\n\nIf you are looking for a specific theorem or inequality linking these four parameters, papers by authors such as **B.P. Mobaraky**, **S.M. Sheikholeslami**, or those focusing on **extremal graph theory** are the primary sources for these mathematical relationships[3][5].\n\nSources:\n[1] [tsinghua.edu.cn](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEVplUNAsl4U7msu-lfx5Xx675-CI98St67lfV2l5MJbxvdj7SMGsxSVTeTWgJjQrA5dTmi9-5L7920XwU3_IorfjfiaanYk03In09st6_8bGN86Nad_9cI5SUs32xAQvRmovvvDR6xKcb7gnhj2D-dvZ5jDygovtiuEf2mKlL8pj00T4swjcc=)\n[2] [unict.it](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEsODsp_ozg9psyiasfQuRGwyF5gNrvUlIlXIUW1SjkemwQBZEFu25ihXSCwmE5r-AVJ5K7Ccglix5Bj_DRVqwIJO6TuVWVO6DweH45R2Zm3mPnWR-LUmtnHfsv4S0SYay_TkCMODHULhHp5mSYJvHz8diWxCXBZdpiXJYwDYxmXTVflyGc1HGCwMqHzhY=)\n[3] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFWf9y83tL0jO1e4yGqmIO1-XQdfgC5V-5wvkJDrNcmghmGVPs29PdZPhgh3QxxSH52hgZeP_2kbMXyruSs1BxFbKnXOKF70WTPRZDOcHqMjl76fMFtN-C_xYPtdCrAOrB35euQS23j1JB2Z7pyY4j1j97Niv0NA4Q-PNs5v5tALxG6Zs6pwDR1XOE1GlZA5HGQg2RTqw==)\n[4] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFWf9y83tL0